=== $\epsilon$-Ball Definition implies $\epsilon$-$\delta$ Definition===
Suppose that [[Definition:Continuous Mapping (Metric Space)/Point/Definition 1|$f$ is $\tuple {d_1, d_2}$-continuous at $a$]] in the sense that:
:$\forall \epsilon \in \R_{>0}: \exists \delta \in \R_{>0}: \map f {\map {B_\delta} {a; d_1} } \subseteq \map {B_\epsilon} {\map f a; d_2}$
where $\map {B_\epsilon} {\map f a; d_2}$ denotes the [[Definition:Open Ball|open $\epsilon$-ball]] of $\map f a$ with respect to the [[Definition:Metric|metric]] $d_2$, and similarly for $\map {B_\delta} {a; d_1}$.
Then:
{{begin-eqn}}
{{eqn | ll= \text{Let}
      | l = \epsilon
      | o = \in
      | r = \R_{>0}
}}
{{eqn | ll= \leadsto
      | l = \exists \delta \in \R_{>0} : \map f {\map {B_\delta} {a; d_1} }
      | o = \subseteq
      | r = \map {B_\epsilon} {\map f a; d_2}
      | c = [[Definition:By Hypothesis|by hypothesis]]
}}
{{eqn | ll= \text{Let}
      | l = x \in A_1
      | o = \text{ such that }
      | r = \map {d_1} {x, a} < \delta
      | c = (*)
}}
{{eqn | ll= \leadsto
      | l = x
      | o = \in
      | r = \map {B_\delta} {a; d_1}
      | c = {{Defof|Open Ball|Open $\epsilon$-Ball}}
}}
{{eqn | ll= \leadsto
      | l = \map f x
      | o = \in
      | r = \map {B_\epsilon} {\map f a; d_2}
      | c = [[Definition:By Hypothesis|by hypothesis]]
}}
{{eqn | ll= \leadsto
      | l = \map {d_2} {\map f x, \map f a}
      | o = <
      | r = \epsilon
      | c = {{Defof|Open Ball|Open $\epsilon$-Ball}}
}}
{{end-eqn}}
{{qed}}
(*) Only consider this case, since otherwise $\map {d_1} {x, a} < \delta \implies \map {d_2} {\map f x, \map f a} < \epsilon$ is [[Definition:Vacuous Truth|vacuously true]].

